1. Definitions

1.1 “Licensor” means NanoDNA Studios.

1.2 “Software” means the software and other materials owned or controlled by the Licensor and specifically identified as covered by this license, including source code, object code, documentation, examples, scripts, configuration files, and original assets. Software does not include third-party materials separately identified or licensed. Third-party materials remain subject to their own terms.

1.3 “Project” means the product, application, service, internal system, or other activity that uses, incorporates, or is operated using the Software. The Project must be identified in the applicable repository, package, or accompanying notice. If no Project is identified, each distinct product, service, or internal system that uses the Software is a separate Project.

1.4 “Business” means a for-profit company, corporation, partnership, sole proprietorship, or other organization or person conducting a profit-seeking activity, and includes a Project owned, controlled, or operated by it. A person using the Software as part of their work for a Business is acting on behalf of that Business, even if the person is not an employee.

1.5 “Related Entity” means an entity that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with a Business. “Control” means direct or indirect ownership of more than 50 percent of voting interests, or other power to direct the entity’s management or operations.

1.6 “People” means each employee and individual contractor working for or on behalf of the Business or any Related Entity. Each person counts as one, regardless of hours worked. A contractor’s employees count as People if they work on behalf of the Business or a Related Entity.

1.7 “Project Revenue” means gross amounts received during the applicable preceding rolling twelve-month period that are directly attributable to the Project, including revenue from sales, subscriptions, paid features, advertising, sponsorships, or paid services connected with the Project. Project Revenue is calculated before expenses and excludes sales taxes collected for remittance and amounts actually refunded to customers. For bundled offerings, only the portion reasonably attributable to the Project is counted. Revenue in another currency must be converted to United States dollars using a consistently applied exchange-rate source and method.

1.8 “Qualifying Small Business” means a Business that, together with its Related Entities:

(a) has no more than 20 People; and

(b) operates a Project with no more than USD $10,000 in Project Revenue during the preceding rolling twelve-month period.

A Business qualifies only while it satisfies both conditions.

1.9 “Institutional User” means a nonprofit organization, school, college, university, research institution, government agency, or other public-sector organization, and any person using the Software on behalf of one of them.

1.10 “You” means the individual or Business exercising rights under this license. An individual acting on behalf of a Business exercises rights only on that Business’s behalf; the individual does not receive a separate personal grant for that use.

1.11 “Separate Written Authorization” means a license or other authorization in a written record issued by the Licensor and expressly accepted by the Licensor. A request, discussion, invoice, or silence is not authorization.

2. Community License Grant

2.1 Subject to this license, the Licensor grants You a worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free, non-transferable license to use, reproduce, modify, and create derivative works of the Software, but only if You are either:

(a) an individual using the Software solely for personal, hobby, educational, or research purposes outside the work or operations of a Business or Institutional User; or

(b) a Qualifying Small Business using the Software solely for its own Project.

2.2 The rights in Section 2.1 include developing, testing, and operating Your own applications or products that use or incorporate the Software, including for payment, while You remain eligible and comply with this license.

2.3 This license does not grant the right to distribute, sublicense, publish, transfer, or otherwise provide the Software, a modified version, or a derivative work containing or based on the Software to another person or entity. Each proposed recipient must first obtain Separate Written Authorization from the Licensor. You must not provide a copy or access before that authorization is in place.

2.4 This license does not authorize use by or on behalf of a Business that does not qualify under Section 1.8, or by an Institutional User, except for the limited transition permissions in Section 5 or a Separate Written Authorization.

2.5 No rights are granted by implication, estoppel, exhaustion, or otherwise beyond those expressly stated in this license.

3. Business and Project Thresholds

3.1 A Business must obtain Separate Written Authorization before continuing use if either:

(a) the Business and its Related Entities have more than 20 People; or

(b) the Project has more than USD $10,000 in Project Revenue during the preceding rolling twelve-month period.

3.2 The People threshold applies to the Business and its Related Entities as a whole, not only to the team working on the Project. The Project Revenue threshold applies to the Project and does not include revenue from unrelated projects, except to the extent it is attributable to the Project under Section 1.7.

3.3 Exceeding either threshold makes the Business ineligible for the community grant even if it remains below the other threshold. The transition period in Section 5 is temporary permission only; it does not make the Business a Qualifying Small Business.

3.4 You are responsible for monitoring eligibility. The Licensor may request reasonable information necessary to confirm eligibility, but this license does not require You to provide personal information beyond what is reasonably necessary for that purpose.

4. Institutional Users

4.1 An Institutional User must obtain Separate Written Authorization before using the Software, except for the temporary permission in Section 5.3.

4.2 The Licensor may decide case by case whether institutional authorization is free or paid and may set its scope, duration, and conditions in writing.

4.3 A request for authorization alone does not grant permission. Any temporary permission while a request is considered is limited to Section 5.3.

5. Temporary Transition and Pending Requests

5.1 Business threshold transition. A Business that first exceeds a threshold in Section 3 may continue using the Software for up to 60 calendar days beginning on the date it first exceeds that threshold, provided it:

(a) gives the Licensor written notice and requests Separate Written Authorization within 30 calendar days after that date; and

(b) continues to comply with this license and does not distribute the Software or provide it to another person or entity.

The 60-day period is a maximum total period measured from the date the threshold was first exceeded. A request or negotiation does not extend it.

5.2 If no request is made. If the Business does not submit its request within the 30-day period, its temporary permission ends at the end of that 30-day period.

5.3 Institutional pending request. An Institutional User that submits a complete written request may use the Software for up to 60 calendar days beginning when the Licensor receives the request, solely for the Project described in it, provided the Institutional User complies with this license and does not distribute the Software or provide it to another person or entity. The Licensor may request reasonable additional information; the 60-day period is not extended by that request.

5.4 End of temporary permission. Temporary permission under this Section ends at the applicable deadline, when Separate Written Authorization takes effect, or when the Licensor gives written notice that the request is denied, whichever occurs first. Negotiations, a delayed response, or a pending request do not extend the deadline. No authorization is granted unless stated in a Separate Written Authorization.

5.5 When temporary permission ends without Separate Written Authorization, You must stop all use that is not otherwise permitted by this license. You may retain archival copies solely as required by law or for routine backup, but must not access or use them.

